# Rules derived from denials measured on a booted device.
#
# Provenance, not subject, is what groups these: each one belongs to a domain
# whose primary definition lives somewhere else (AOSP's policy, or the QTI
# vendor policy in device/qcom/sepolicy_vndr), so there is no natural .te file
# of ours to put it in. Everything here comes from work/s15/boot1 — a 240 s
# capture from t=0 on a permissive boot — and nothing here was copied from the
# stock policy or inferred.
#
# What is deliberately NOT here, so the next session does not go looking:
#
#   * The ~2200 `zygote`/`hal_camera_default` -> *_prop { open getattr map }
#     denials. Those are one process each enumerating the whole property area
#     once at startup (__system_property_foreach maps every context file). A
#     property a domain cannot read simply reads back empty, every domain has
#     explicit get_prop() for the ones it needs, and "fix" would mean ~700 new
#     grants. Noise.
#
#   * Denials whose SOURCE is a core domain — system_app on sysfs_zram,
#     priv_app on proc_version, system_server mapping libsdmextension.so,
#     servicemanager -> hal_graphics_composer_default, system_suspend on a
#     sysfs `name`, init on debugfs_bootreceiver_tracing, kernel's
#     net_bind_service. BOARD_VENDOR_SEPOLICY_DIRS cannot add rules for
#     coredomains, and it should not: those are upstream's to fix.
#
#   * vendor_init setting ro.system.build.fingerprint / ro.bootimage.build
#     .fingerprint. ro.* is write-once and init has already set both from
#     build.prop by then, so the write is a guaranteed no-op; granting a vendor
#     domain write access to build_prop to silence a cosmetic denial is a real
#     privilege widening for nothing.

#####################################
# Thermal engine's client sockets.
#
#   avc: denied { create setattr } name="thermal-send-client"
#        scontext=u:r:vendor_thermal-engine:s0
#        tcontext=u:object_r:socket_device:s0 tclass=sock_file
#
# init_thermal-engine-v2.rc declares four of these (thermal-send-client,
# thermal-recv-client, thermal-recv-passive-client, thermal-send-rule) and the
# daemon re-creates them itself; on the running device all four are
# u:object_r:socket_device:s0, i.e. they keep /dev/socket's own label.
#
# This is not cosmetic. Those sockets are the command channel that
# thermal-switch-engine uses to move between the seven thermal-engine-malbec-*
# policies. Lose them under enforcing and the device is stuck in whichever
# profile it booted with, which on this build is the weakest one.
allow vendor_thermal-engine socket_device:sock_file { create setattr unlink };

#####################################
# Lenovo/Tinno hardware inventory node.
#
#   avc: denied { read write open } name="info_gsensor"
#        scontext=u:r:hal_sensors_default:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:sysfs:s0
#
# /sys/devices/platform/product-device-info/ is a per-component inventory the
# ODM's drivers and HALs fill in at probe. Read off the running device:
#
#   info_gsensor  LSM6DSVETR_ACCEL      info_gyro   LSM6DSVETR_GYRO
#   info_hall     OCH87893EV1AD         info_lsensor GS6155
#   info_sar      AW96305DNR            info_fingerprint and info_nfc EMPTY
#
# (The two empty ones are worth recording: they are the ODM's own statement
# that this unit has neither part, matching the tree's existing conclusions.)
#
# The sensors HAL both reads and writes its entry, so this is r+w, but only for
# this one directory rather than the sysfs blanket stock uses.
type sysfs_product_device_info, fs_type, sysfs_type;
allow hal_sensors_default sysfs_product_device_info:dir search;
allow hal_sensors_default sysfs_product_device_info:file rw_file_perms;

#####################################
# Gatekeeper reading KeyMint's retry timer.
#
#   avc: denied { read open getattr map }
#        scontext=u:r:vendor_hal_gatekeeper_qti:s0
#        tcontext=u:object_r:vendor_keymint_retry_timer_prop:s0 tclass=file
#
# The QTI gatekeeper shares the throttling counter with KeyMint. Without this
# the lockout timer after failed unlock attempts reads as unset.
get_prop(vendor_hal_gatekeeper_qti, vendor_keymint_retry_timer_prop)

#####################################
# NOT granted: hal_lenovobattery_default reading persist.debug.*
#
#   avc: denied { read open getattr map } scontext=u:r:hal_lenovobattery_default:s0
#        tcontext=u:object_r:persist_debug_prop:s0 tclass=file
#
# This was written as a get_prop() and the build rejected it outright:
#
#   neverallow on line 291 of system/sepolicy/private/property.te violated by
#   allow hal_lenovobattery_default persist_debug_prop:file { read open }
#
# ⚠️ The "line 291 of system/sepolicy/private/property.te" above is the
# compiler's own message, quoted verbatim. It means THAT file -- not the
# property.te sitting next to this file, which is 38 lines long. Left as the
# compiler printed it, with this note, because a rewritten error message is not
# an error message. The line number is from the session that hit it and that
# file is now 908 lines; do not chase it.
#
# ⚠️ And do not conclude too much from the sentence that used to be here: "the
# { domain -coredomain } form -- no vendor domain may touch this type, and no
# rule anywhere can grant it". The REJECTION is real and reproducible, and that
# is what matters. The reason given is doubtful: persist_debug_prop is declared
# `system_restricted_prop` (system/sepolicy/public/property.te:124, inside the
# compatible_property_only block that applies here), and restricted props are
# exactly the ones a vendor domain CAN be granted with an explicit allow --
# :175's neverallow excludes -system_restricted_property_type. Something else
# in that file is what fired. Worth ten minutes in the enforcing pass, because
# if it is grantable the denial is fixable; not worth it now, for the reason
# below. Same shape as the set_prop(prop_init, system_prop) attempt that was
# rejected in session 12.
#
# The cost is nil: persist.debug.* is a debug toggle, and the HAL reads it to
# decide whether to log more. Leaving it denied means it logs less.

#####################################
# Face unlock allocating from the system dmabuf heap.
#
#   avc: denied { read open } name="system"
#        scontext=u:r:hal_faceunlock_default:s0
#        tcontext=u:object_r:dmabuf_system_heap_device:s0 tclass=chr_file
#
# Standard for any HAL that has to allocate graphics/ION buffers on Android 12+.
allow hal_faceunlock_default dmabuf_system_heap_device:chr_file r_file_perms;

#####################################
# Parallel module loading.
#
#   avc: denied { write }      scontext=vendor_modprobe tcontext=vendor_modprobe tclass=key
#   avc: denied { sys_nice }   scontext=vendor_modprobe tcontext=vendor_modprobe tclass=capability
#   avc: denied { setsched }   scontext=vendor_modprobe tcontext=kernel        tclass=process
#
# androidboot.load_modules_parallel=true is set in this device's bootconfig, so
# modprobe forks workers and renices them. All three are the standard cost of
# that flag.
allow vendor_modprobe self:key write;
allow vendor_modprobe self:capability sys_nice;
allow vendor_modprobe kernel:process setsched;

#####################################
# prop_init reading /proc/meminfo.
#
#   avc: denied { read open getattr } name="meminfo" scontext=u:r:prop_init:s0
#        tcontext=u:object_r:proc_meminfo:s0 tclass=file
#
# prop_init publishes a RAM-size property alongside the serials and MACs.
allow prop_init proc_meminfo:file r_file_perms;

#####################################
# The CABL/SVI shell scripts reading Lenovo's init property.
#
#   avc: denied { read open getattr map } scontext=u:r:vendor_qti_init_shell:s0
#        tcontext=u:object_r:vendor_lenovotb_init_prop:s0 tclass=file
get_prop(vendor_qti_init_shell, vendor_lenovotb_init_prop)

#####################################
# USB HAL walking /sys/class/qcom-battery.
#
#   avc: denied { search } comm="android.hardwar" name="qcom-battery"
#        scontext=u:r:vendor_hal_usb_qti:s0
#        tcontext=u:object_r:sysfs_qcom_battery:s0 tclass=dir
#
# ⚠️ This one is self-inflicted and only appeared after flashing. That directory
# used to be plain sysfs, which vendor_hal_usb_qti can already search; giving it
# its own type (for the charging-control gate) took the access away. It reads
# usb_real_type / usb_typec_compliant from there.
#
# The lesson generalises: splitting a type out of a generic one is not additive.
# Anything that reached the old label loses the new one, and a permissive boot is
# where you find out.
#
# ⚠️ And READ IS NOT ENOUGH — this was restored as read-only and the HAL writes.
# android.hardware.usb-service.qti is built FROM SOURCE here (device.mk), and
# vendor/qcom/opensource/usb/hal/Usb.cpp's limitPowerTransfer() does
#     :1298  WriteStringToFile("0", "/sys/class/qcom-battery/restrict_cur")
#     :1304  WriteStringToFile("1", "/sys/class/qcom-battery/restrict_chg")
#     :1310  WriteStringToFile("0", "/sys/class/qcom-battery/restrict_chg")
# Both nodes are real on this unit (work/device_dump/stock/power/qcom-battery.txt).
# It worked on plain sysfs only because system/sepolicy/private/hal_usb.te:17-20
# grants `allow hal_usb sysfs:file { read open write getattr }` — on the GENERIC
# type. Under enforcing every branch of limitPowerTransfer would return
# Status::ERROR and USB power limiting / contaminant-detection charge
# restriction would silently stop applying.
#
# rw_dir_file subsumes both the search and the r_dir_file above.
allow vendor_hal_usb_qti sysfs_qcom_battery:dir search;
rw_dir_file(vendor_hal_usb_qti, sysfs_qcom_battery)

#####################################
# Property-area enumeration — dontaudit, and this one is a judgement call worth
# writing down because "just allow it" would be a real security regression.
#
# 790 of the 842 AVC records in work/s16/boot0 are the same shape: one process
# doing getattr/open/map on EVERY file under /dev/__properties__, i.e. on all
# ~280 property types at once. Two independent callers, not one:
#
#   u:r:zygote:s0             193 getattr + 272 open + 183 map, comm="main"
#   u:r:hal_camera_default:s0  49 getattr +  52 open +  49 map, comm="binder:N_2"
#
# Both are __system_property_foreach / property_list() walks — a library asking
# "what properties exist" rather than reading any particular one. On the zygote
# side it is the Vulkan preload (ro.hwui.use_vulkan=true) pulling in
# vulkan.adreno.so; on the camera side it is libcamxsettingsmanager.so via
# libcamxcommonutils.so. Both are vendor blobs we do not build and cannot patch.
#
# Why dontaudit and not allow: granting the access would mean ~280 get_prop()
# lines per domain, handing zygote — the ancestor of every app process — read
# access to the entire property space, including types the platform
# deliberately keeps away from it. The enumeration ALREADY works: it is a walk
# that skips what it cannot open, and both callers behave correctly today
# (camera takes pictures, Vulkan initialises). Under enforcing they will keep
# behaving correctly, just with fewer entries visible. So there is nothing to
# fix — only an audit record to stop emitting.
#
# ⚠️ This also unmasks the rest of the log. With these 790 records gone the boot
# has ~50 AVC records instead of 842, and the audit backlog stops overflowing —
# the s16 capture hit the kernel's 64-record burst limit, so the 842 was itself
# an undercount and some real denials were dropped on the floor.
#
# ⚠️ `read` is deliberately NOT in these rules, and it was before. The evidence
# recorded above counts three permissions only -- getattr, open, map -- for both
# domains, and `read` appeared in neither tally. In a file called measured.te
# whose whole method is "only what was measured", the extra permission was the
# one thing here that was not.
#
# It matters more for zygote than the width suggests: zygote is the ancestor of
# every app process, so silencing a whole permission for it against EVERY
# property type is the widest possible suppression in the worst possible domain,
# and it would hide a real future failure of an app to read a property it needs.
# That defeats the "unmasks the rest of the log" purpose stated just above.
dontaudit zygote property_type:file { getattr open map };
dontaudit hal_camera_default property_type:file { getattr open map };

# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Session 24 — the residue, and the decision to be quieter than stock rather
# than more permissive than stock.
#
# Owner's question, and it is the right one: "stock has these denials too; our
# system is simpler, so it should be able to be cleaner than stock." It can.
# Below is every denial left on a clean boot that involves a VENDOR domain or a
# VENDOR type — i.e. the part this port actually owns — with a decision each.
#
# ★ THE RULE APPLIED THROUGHOUT: `dontaudit` when nothing is lost, a narrow
# `allow` only when a function is. Granting a permission to silence a log line
# is how a policy rots, and #54b is this session's worked example of the cost —
# stock's `allow` there exists to let a chmod 0777 succeed on the partition
# holding this unit's serials.
#
# ⚠️ What is deliberately NOT here: the AOSP-wide core-to-core denials
# (`kernel:capability net_bind_service` from krfcommd, `init ->
# debugfs_bootreceiver_tracing`, `isolated_app -> content_capture_service`,
# `priv_app -> metadata_file`, `fsck`/`e2fs -> sysfs` while formatting a wiped
# /data). Those are identical on a Pixel, are not ours, and silencing them in a
# DEVICE policy would hide a real regression in AOSP from the next person to
# read this log. The target this file aims at is precise: **zero denials
# involving a vendor domain or a vendor type**, not zero lines.
#
# ⚠️ And these rules are only reachable because vendor policy CAN write rules
# whose source is a coredomain. `measured.te:20-24` used to say it cannot;
# device/qcom/sepolicy_vndr/sm8750/generic/vendor/common/surfaceflinger.te:32-35
# does exactly that (`dontaudit surfaceflinger firmware_file:dir search;` and an
# `allow surfaceflinger vendor_sysfs_graphics:file rw_file_perms;`), and this
# build proves it. The correction is recorded in OPEN-ISSUES.md #58.

# 1. Properties this device's own init deliberately fails to set.
#
# Both are documented at length in vendor_init.te and daemons.te as impossible
# to succeed (ro.* is write-once and build.prop has already set them) AND
# undesirable if they did (Lenovo's per-unit fingerprint rewrite would overwrite
# the one lineage_malbec.mk manages). They are pure noise: expected, permanent,
# and one line each per boot.
dontaudit vendor_init build_prop:property_service set;
dontaudit vendor_init build_bootimage_prop:property_service set;
dontaudit prop_init system_prop:property_service set;
dontaudit prop_init serialno_prop:property_service set;

# 2. Vendor daemons walking the property space.
#
# Same shape as the zygote/hal_camera pair above, and the same reasoning: these
# are enumerations that skip what they cannot open. QTI's own policy already
# establishes the pattern for exactly this type —
# sepolicy_vndr/sm8750/generic/vendor/common/hal_camera.te:99 is
# `dontaudit hal_camera_default default_prop:file read;` and vm_mgr.te:58 the
# same for its own domain. Ours needs the other three permissions of the same
# access, from /vendor/bin/tn_vendor_performance.sh's property reads.
dontaudit vendor_qti_init_shell default_prop:file { getattr open map read };

# 3. Debug and config properties that nothing needs.
#
#   hal_lenovobattery_default -> persist_debug_prop   a persist.debug.* toggle
#   hal_camera_default        -> usb_uvc_config_prop  UVC (USB webcam) config
#
# Neither is a feature. The battery HAL logs less; the camera HAL reads an empty
# value for a USB-camera path this tablet does not have. Both are denied on stock
# too, so nothing regresses relative to the shipping image — the difference is
# only that ours stops saying so eight times a boot.
dontaudit hal_lenovobattery_default persist_debug_prop:file { getattr open map read };
dontaudit hal_camera_default usb_uvc_config_prop:file { getattr open map read };

# 4. The composer's stat of /mnt/vendor.
#
# ★ The one `allow` in this block, because this one IS an access rather than an
# enumeration: the composer stats the mount point before deciding whether its
# per-unit display calibration is available (/mnt/vendor/persist/display/ holds
# panel_csot_nt36536_144hz_vid_C3D_cali_{srgb,p3}.txt on this unit, and they are
# real). `getattr` on the directory is the narrowest thing that answers the
# question; it grants no read of anything inside.
allow hal_graphics_composer_default mnt_vendor_file:dir getattr;

# 5. Coredomain clients of vendor services that stock does not grant either.
#
#   surfaceflinger, bootanim -> vendor_hal_qspmhal_default : binder call
#   servicemanager           -> hal_graphics_composer_default : binder call
#   system_server            -> vendor_file : file { getattr open read map }
#
# All three are denied by the shipping ZUI image as well — checked by grepping
# its compiled vendor_sepolicy.cil, which has 25 references to
# vendor_hal_qspmhal_default and not one `allow ... (binder (call))` for it.
#
# ⚠️ NOT granted, and the asymmetry with item 4 is the point. Granting these
# means widening a CORE domain's reach into vendor space:
#   * `system_server -> vendor_file` was listed here too and that was WRONG —
#     see the note below. It is a real access, it is fixable, and it is now fixed
#     by relabelling rather than silenced.
#   * the two binder calls are QTI's own gap in QTI's own HALs, and the effect is
#     a power hint that is not delivered — on stock either.
# So: silence, do not widen. If any of these ever turns out to cost a measurable
# feature, the fix is a narrower TYPE, not a broader rule.
dontaudit surfaceflinger vendor_hal_qspmhal_default:binder call;
dontaudit bootanim vendor_hal_qspmhal_default:binder call;
dontaudit servicemanager hal_graphics_composer_default:binder call;
# ⚠️ `dontaudit system_server vendor_file:file { getattr open read map };` was
# here and has been REMOVED, because it was the one rule in this block that hid
# something real. Measured after it was written: system_server has
# libsdmextension.so MAPPED on the running device, so enforcing would have taken
# a working library away and this line would have deleted the only warning.
# It is fixed properly instead — sepolicy/vendor/file_contexts labels that one
# file `same_process_hal_file`, which is AOSP's type for a vendor library loaded
# into a core process, and AOSP already grants system_server access to it.

# 6. vold enumerating vendor partitions it does not manage.
#
# /mnt/vendor/spunvm and /mnt/vendor/qmcs are mounted from the vendor fstab by
# init, not by vold; vold walks the mount table at startup and stats what it
# finds. Denying it changes nothing — both are already mounted and both are
# still mounted after the denial (verified live: /proc/mounts has them).
dontaudit vold vendor_spunvm_file:dir { getattr ioctl open read };
dontaudit vold vendor_qmcs_file:dir { getattr ioctl open read };

# 7. The QTI CA-certificate service dexopting at runtime.
#
#   vendor_cacertservice_app -> system_data_file : dir { add_name create }
#   on .../cache/oat_primary/arm64/CACertService.art
#
# ⚠️ `dontaudit` rather than `allow`, deliberately, and the cost is stated: under
# enforcing this app runs interpreted instead of AOT-compiled. That is the right
# trade here — it is a certificate service that runs rarely, and the alternative
# is granting an APP domain create/add_name on system_data_file directories,
# which is precisely the boundary AOSP's app-domain neverallows exist to hold.
# Stock denies it identically, so stock's copy runs interpreted too.
#
# The better fix, if anyone wants it, is upstream of this file: give the app a
# seapp_contexts entry so its cache lands on an app data type instead of
# system_data_file. Not done here because it changes where an existing app's
# data lives, which is not a change to make on a whim.
dontaudit vendor_cacertservice_app system_data_file:dir { add_name create };
